    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Here, factors 1 and 2 are completely confounded, as they contribute equally to all individuals, so unique estimates of $ \\beta_{1} $ and $ \\beta_{2} $ cannot be acquired. Generally, when two or more columns of X are not independent, it is still possible to obtain unique BLUEs for certain linear combinations of $ \\beta $ through the use of generalized inverses (Appendix 3). With the preceding design matrix X, the solution is simple—by combining the two factors into a single new factor, $ \\beta_{1} + \\beta_{2} $, the new model becomes",
